<h2>Stream Separation & Signal Handling</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Clean <code>stdout</code> contract:</strong> When <code>--json</code> is specified, <code>stdout</code> receives exactly one valid UTF-8 JSON document upon completion. All warnings, notices, and live progress stay on <code>stderr</code> so pipelines such as <code>flux encode ... --json | jq .</code> never break.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong><code>NO_COLOR</code> & non-TTY detection:</strong> ANSI colour and cursor control sequences are suppressed when <code>NO_COLOR</code> is set, <code>--no-color</code> is passed, or output is redirected.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Graceful <code>SIGINT</code> / <code>SIGTERM</code> cancellation:</strong> Both <code>Ctrl+C</code> (<code>SIGINT</code>) and termination signals (<code>SIGTERM</code> from <code>kill</code>, systemd, or container runtimes) trigger a two-stage shutdown:
    <ol>
      <li>First signal: FluxEncoder writes <code>q</code> to FFmpeg's standard input, waits up to the grace period for FFmpeg to close its file trailer, removes any incomplete <code>.partial</code> file (unless <code>--keep-partial</code>), restores terminal cursor state, and exits with code <code>130</code>.</li>
      <li>Second signal: Terminates the process immediately.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>
</ul>

